# Every text file is normalized to LF in the index and checked out with LF, so a
# Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true gets the same bytes as everyone else. The
# size gate depends on it: it counts newlines and rejects any CR byte in a source
# file, which without this line a CRLF checkout would turn into a violation for
# every file in the tree. text=auto leaves git's binary detection in charge, so
# images and archives are untouched. The specific rules below still win, since a
# later pattern beats an earlier one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Force LF on files that must stay LF inside the Docker image.
#
# The container ENTRYPOINT is docker-entrypoint.sh. If a contributor checks the
# repo out on Windows with core.autocrlf=true (the platform default), the script
# is rewritten to CRLF on disk, COPYed into the image verbatim, and its shebang
# becomes "#!/bin/sh\r". The kernel then tries to exec the interpreter "/bin/sh\r",
# which does not exist, and the container dies at startup. Pinning eol=lf here makes
# the checkout LF regardless of platform or autocrlf setting, fixing it at the source.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Git hooks live in scripts/ (core.hooksPath=scripts) and are extensionless
# bash scripts, so the *.sh rule above misses them. A CRLF checkout would break
# their shebang and silently disable the hooks on Windows.
scripts/* text eol=lf

# Files whose exact bytes matter more than the convention, so the rule above skips
# them entirely rather than converting them:
#
# gradlew.bat is Gradle's Windows launcher and cmd.exe wants CRLF. The two OFL
# texts and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md carry upstream licence wording verbatim, and
# the notices file is regenerated and diffed by CI (`make notices`), so a
# normalized copy in the index would never match what the generator writes.
android/gradlew.bat -text
android/licenses/* -text
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md -text

# A pattern containing a slash is anchored and its * never matches a /, so the
# rule above covers scripts/ itself and nothing deeper, while a slashless pattern
# like *.sh matches at any depth. The size gate parses its allowlist as
# path<TAB>lines and reads a trailing CR as part of the number, so a CRLF checkout
# turns every entry into a parse error. Pin the file the parser reads by full path.
scripts/ci/size-allowlist.txt text eol=lf
